{"issue":{"title":"csv2json: add a --tsv flag to read tab-separated input","problem":"bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and always calls parse(text) from src/parse.js with its default ',' separator; it ignores all arguments. Today `printf 'a\\tb\\n1,5\\tx\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` exits 0 and prints [{\"a\\tb\":\"1\"}] — the tab is not treated as a separator and the comma inside a field splits it. src/parse.js already accepts an optional separator (parse(text, sep = ',')), but the CLI never passes one. README.md documents only CSV usage.","reason":"Users now receive exports as TSV and must pipe them through sed to turn tabs into commas before csv2json, which is an extra step and corrupts any field that contains a comma.","outcome":"`node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` reads tab-separated text on stdin and prints the same JSON array of header-keyed objects it prints for CSV; running without the flag behaves exactly as before, and the shared parse() export keeps its current comma default.","expect":["`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` exits 0 and prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.","With --tsv, commas inside fields are kept as data: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1,5\\tx\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1,5\",\"b\":\"x\"}]` followed by a newline.","Without the flag, CSV behaviour is unchanged: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline, and `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` still prints `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline (tabs are not separators by default).","The exported parse() from src/parse.js still defaults to commas: `node --input-type=module -e \"import {parse} from './src/parse.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(parse('a,b\\n1,2\\n')), JSON.stringify(parse('a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n','\\t')))\"` prints `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`.","`npm test` exits 0, and the test suite in test/ includes at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and asserts its JSON output.","README.md documents the --tsv flag with a usage example."],"proofs":[{"line":"`printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` exits 0 and prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.","proof":"run","how":"printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit $?\""},{"line":"With --tsv, commas inside fields are kept as data: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1,5\\tx\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1,5\",\"b\":\"x\"}]` followed by a newline.","proof":"run","how":"printf 'a\\tb\\n1,5\\tx\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv"},{"line":"Without the flag, CSV behaviour is unchanged: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline, and `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` still prints `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline (tabs are not separators by default).","proof":"run","how":"printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js; printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js"},{"line":"The exported parse() from src/parse.js still defaults to commas: `node --input-type=module -e \"import {parse} from './src/parse.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(parse('a,b\\n1,2\\n')), JSON.stringify(parse('a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n','\\t')))\"` prints `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`.","proof":"run","how":"From the repo root: node --input-type=module -e \"import {parse} from './src/parse.js'; console.log(JSON.stringify(parse('a,b\\n1,2\\n')), JSON.stringify(parse('a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n','\\t')))\""},{"line":"`npm test` exits 0, and the test suite in test/ includes at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and asserts its JSON output.","proof":"run","how":"npm test; echo \"exit $?\"; then grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js and read the matching test"},{"line":"README.md documents the --tsv flag with a usage example.","proof":"read","how":"Read README.md and confirm it shows a command using --tsv and says it reads tab-separated input"}],"non_goals":["Quoted fields, escaped separators or any other RFC 4180 / full TSV quoting support.","A generic --delimiter/--sep option or auto-detection of the separator.","Changing the default separator of parse() or the default CSV behaviour of the CLI.","Publishing a release or bumping the package version."],"assumptions":["The flag is spelled exactly --tsv and only switches the field separator to a tab; header handling, trimming, missing-cell filling and output format stay as for CSV.","The existing parse(text, sep) export in src/parse.js is shared and its comma default must not change; how the CLI passes the tab separator is left to the builder.","Behaviour for unknown flags is left unchanged/unspecified, since the request does not mention it."]}}